        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Ok I just did some research on this and based on this comment from nvim-dap author itself https://github.com/mfussenegger/nvim-dap/discussions/810#discussioncomment-4623606, there is currently an internal api like ``require('dap.breakpoints').get()`` but the cavecat is that it is internal and not meant for external use and its arguments can change anytime without notice since it is internal. \r\n\r\nAlso about scopes and frames there is already dap.ui.widgets for that and they can be used to show both scopes and frames using the following remaps.\r\n\r\n```lua\t\r\nNmap(\"<leader>ds\", \":lua require('dap.ui.widgets').centered_float(require('dap.ui.widgets'.scopes)<CR>\", \"Scopes\")\r\nNmap(\"<leader>df\", \":lua require('dap.ui.widgets').centered_float(require('dap.ui.widgets').frames)<CR>\", \"Frames\")\r\n```\r\nThe first one is for scopes and the second one is for frames.\r\n\r\nAs per breakpoints we have ``require(\"dap\").list_breakpoints()`` which will populate quickfix list with all the breakpoint lines.\r\n\r\nWe also have ``require(\"dap\").clear_breakpoints()`` to clear all breakpoints. \r\n\r\nSo the clever way to deal with specific bulk breakpoint toggling is probably to delete unecessary items in the quickfix list and just use some command like `cdo` and call the toggle_breakpoint function on that line is probably the way to go.\r\n\r\nSince the quickfix list is populated when we call list_breakpoints() method, we can probably view it using mini.pick using something like ``MiniExtra.pickers.list({scope = \"quickfix\"})``.\r\n\r\n\r\n",
